osition; provider=codex; schema=1; producer=v1.6.1; diagnostic=launch_composition_empty; no project MCP servers enabled\nspawn queued: [reviewer] reviewer (codex) (run=RUN-260831-c34bb4, max_parallel=20)\nspawn run started: [reviewer] reviewer (codex) (run=RUN-260831-c34bb4)\nagent completed: [reviewer] reviewer (codex) (exit=0)\nspawn run completed: codex (run=RUN-260831-c34bb4, pid=4457, exit=0)\nCR revision 1 blocking finding accepted and it is the most serious defect found in this Story. CalculateObjectIdentity and VerifyObjectIdentity validate generic JSON, common-model numbers, the schema lookup and the self-field digest, but never the selected schemas closed member set or nested contract invariants. The reviewer drove both production entries with three invalid Blob Descriptors: an unknown top-level member, an unknown member inside a nested BlobChunk, and a chunk with index=9 offset=99 size=0 for a one-byte blob. All three received a digest and all three verified. For a content-addressed identity that is a bypass around the check: the system attests an object the closed contract requires the receiver to reject. Rework must add a scoped closed-shape validator for Sections 10.1-10.4 covering exact top-level and nested member sets plus chunk order, bounds and coverage, and it must be invoked on the composed production path rather than tested as an isolated helper. Separately the assigned Section 17.3 still has no scoped implementation owner and must be made traceable without claiming migration publication or atomic-reference behavior that README correctly reports unavailable.\nSTORY-260830-2wdm5e base refresh SKIPPED: the managed workspace holds uncommitted work, so there was no clean checkpoint branch to replay onto trunk e8f8e2febfbc; the branch is unchanged at fork point c9e5290b1506\nspawn workload selection: class=implementation source=derived policy=spawn.workload_classes pair=codex/gpt-5.6-sol/high pair_source=explicit match=recommended_rank_1 snapshot=sha